Physics, part 1: an in-process (half-cut) stock model.

- Mill jobs may set `stock` (a box in the part frame) and `stock_model` (`cell`, `enabled`). The model is on by default when `stock` is set, and existing jobs are unchanged.
- The stock is a multi-interval Z-dexel grid (crates/mesh/src/stock.rs). Each cell keeps a list of solid Z intervals, so undercuts left by tilted tools are kept. A sphere and a finite cylinder at any orientation are subtracted exactly on each dexel ray. A straight cut with a constant tool axis also subtracts the exact swept cylinder. Rotating axes, arcs and the ball nose are sampled with the same rules as the collision check.
- `campanion run` replays the toolpaths through the stock:
  - a rapid that enters the remaining stock fails the run;
  - a shank, holder or head that meets the current stock fails the run (the finished-part and fixture checks stay);
  - after an STL program, the run prints the maximum and mean leftover and a model-gouge count.
- Randomized (proptest) tests cover:
  - the ray intervals against point sampling;
  - the swept volume against the analytic value;
  - agreement with the drop-cutter gouge check;
  - agreement with `stock_to_leave` and the roughing step-down;
  - rapid detection;
  - order invariance.
- New example `examples/dome_stock.job.json`, with an e2e test for the rapid-into-stock error.

Steep walls are only as sharp as the cell size. This output has not been proven on a machine.

